  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02489v1</id>
    <title>Cross-Domain Joint-Embedding Pretraining For Heterogeneous Industrial Time Series</title>
    <updated>2026-11-02T04:05:47Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02489v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.02489v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Industrial predictive maintenance and manufacturing tasks frequently contain abundant sensor measurements, but with limited labeled data. We investigate cross-domain self-supervised pretraining using joint-embedding predictive architecture for heterogeneous industrial time series (JEPA-HITS), which learns one encoder from various domains such as battery, semiconductor chemical mechanical planarization (CMP), computer numerical control (CNC) machining, and bearing while preserving dataset-specific temporal scales. We evaluate representation reuse on regression and classification problems under reduced-label downstream training, and leave-one-domain-out (LODO) pretraining. Compared with the same architecture initialized randomly, LODO pretraining reduces RMSE from 10.72 to 7.95 on battery remaining useful life (RUL), 4.34 to 3.89 on CMP, and 13.53 to 11.72 on tool wear regression, while improving bearing fault diagnosis macro-F1 from 0.547 to 0.737. Overall results suggest that unlabeled time series measurements from heterogeneous industrial processes can provide reusable representations for downstream learning with limited labeled data.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-02T04:05:47Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Submitted to International Conference on Acoustics, Speech, and Signal Processing (ICASSP) on Sept. 24, 2026</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Jonathan Adam Rico</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Nagarajan Raghavan</name>
    </author>
