  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40356v1</id>
    <title>JEDI: Joint-Embedding Demonstration Compression for In-Context Imitation Learning</title>
    <updated>2026-10-29T16:11:05Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40356v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.40356v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>In-context imitation learning (ICIL) adapts robot policies through test-time demonstrations, but long multimodal trajectories constrain how many examples fit in context. We ask what a compressed demonstration should preserve. We propose JEDI, which compresses demonstrations into latent slots trained to predict the query episode's next-stage keyframe in a frozen video-JEPA space. Task and process counterfactuals help the slots preserve execution details, while the predictor selects demonstrations from a bank. Post-training with keypoint tokens enables human-hand conditioning on robots. Experiments on LIBERO and real robots show that JEDI supports many-shot context; LIBERO objective controls show improved success and demonstration following over action-only compression. These findings support query-future prediction as a training objective for preserving execution-relevant information in compressed demonstrations.</summary>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-29T16:11:05Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.RO"/>
    <author>
      <name>Jiazhao Liang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zhewen He</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Shuaihang Yuan</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Hao Huang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yi Fang</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.38508v1</id>
    <title>SaiVLA-JEPA: Evaluating Future Latent Prediction in JEPA-Conditioned VLA Control</title>
    <updated>2026-10-28T09:25:24Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.38508v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.38508v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Joint-embedding predictive architectures (JEPAs) learn to predict representations rather than pixels, offering a compact way to model how visual scenes evolve. We ask whether this predictive objective improves closed-loop control when a vision-language-action (VLA) policy already uses V-JEPA features. We call our JEPA-conditioned flow policy family SaiVLA-JEPA. It combines a vision-language model with a diffusion transformer action expert and receives features from a frozen V-JEPA encoder. Two lightweight auxiliary heads predict future representations during training and are omitted at inference, leaving the action sampler unchanged. We compare policies without JEPA features, with current JEPA conditioning, and with additional future supervision on 10,030 paired LIBERO-Plus episodes. Current JEPA conditioning reaches about 82.5% success versus 71.6% without JEPA, while future supervision provides no detectable aggregate gain at the evaluated training budget. We separately evaluate SaiVLA-JEPA-TCA, which adapts latent context, and SaiVLA-JEPA-TPA, which adapts selected parameters. The context-retry gain is a system-level result under a larger attempt budget; parameter adaptation shows no consistent advantage over the frozen policy and target controls. These results motivate assessing latent-prediction objectives through paired closed-loop rollouts rather than latent loss alone.</summary>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-28T09:25:24Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.RO"/>
    <author>
      <name>Rufeng Xiao</name>
      <arxiv:affiliation>VLA Research Team, Synthoid.ai</arxiv:affiliation>
    </author>
    <author>
